---
/**
 * One product row of the Footer's cross-sell list.
 *
 * INTERNAL, and the folder is the whole point. `astro-components/components/`
 * is public surface — `check:exports` requires every .astro in it to be in the
 * barrel, to have a README section, and to move the "N components" count. This
 * row is not a component anyone should reach for on its own; it exists so the
 * docs can render ONE row in its rest and hover states instead of keeping a
 * hand-written copy of it, which is what they did until 2026-08-13 and which
 * had already drifted — the copy still carried `leading-[1.5]` and a
 * page-local `--primary` alias while Footer had moved on twice.
 *
 * No script names this folder any more. Every one of them asks
 * `scripts/lib/sources.mjs`, which walks the package and returns `internal` as
 * DATA. This paragraph used to say "two scripts must know this folder exists"
 * and name them; by 2026-08-26 the answer was four, and nothing could have
 * said so — a count in prose is a fact nothing checks. Worse, the scripts that
 * did NOT know silently skipped this file, which is how `check:parity` stopped
 * asking about `FlyoutMenu` the day it moved here.
 *
 * ── The row this component declares, and what draws it ────────────────────
 *
 * The 28 below is the whole reason `Footer`'s band was 470 for as long as it
 * was, and it is the number that had NO check: ten rows agreeing with each
 * other at 27 looked exactly like ten rows agreeing at 28. It is declared here
 * rather than on Footer because this file is where it is spent — the band is
 * arithmetic over it (10 x 28 + 9 x 8 = 352, + 64 x 2 = 480), and `Footer`
 * declares both ends of that sum, so a row that moves cannot move quietly.
 *
 * Twice, at 10 and at 1, because the docs render this row in two places and the
 * hover pair is a hand-forced state — a row that is 28 in the list and
 * something else under `.demo-row-hover` is a docs bug that renders as spec.
 *
 * @figma-size row
 *   node   507:3253
 *   frame  Product footer
 *   page   component-footer.html
 *   region default
 *   select li
 *   at     1440
 *   count  10
 *   height 28
 *   why    size/xl 20 + leading/7 28 over Typography/body 16/28; was 27
 *
 * @figma-size row-hover
 *   node   507:3253
 *   frame  Product footer
 *   page   component-footer.html
 *   region hover-active
 *   select li
 *   at     1440
 *   height 28
 *   why    the forced-hover copy; only the colours move, never the box
 *
 * The mark is NOT declared, and the gate is why. It is `h-[24px]`, a literal in
 * this file, so 10px of injected padding cannot move it and arm 3 refuses the
 * reading rather than passing it — which is the arm behaving. `figma-size` is
 * for a height nobody wrote down: one that EMERGES from type, spacing and
 * content, where ten elements agreeing prove nothing. A hard-coded box already
 * carries its number in source where a reader can see it.
 */
export interface Product {
  prefix: string;   // e.g. "IRON"
  suffix: string;   // e.g. "PDF"
  desc: string;
  accent?: string;  // CSS color for the suffix, e.g. "var(--iron-blue-500)"
  href?: string;
}

interface Props {
  product: Product;
  /** Already-encoded path to the 22px mark that fades in on hover. */
  logoSrc: string;
  /**
   * Lands on the <li>. The docs page uses it to force the hover illustration —
   * `.demo-row-hover img` and `.demo-row-hover .text-text-dark-support` — since
   * :hover cannot be rendered statically. Footer itself passes nothing.
   */
  class?: string;
}

const { product: p, logoSrc, class: className } = Astro.props;
---

{/* EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS THE NODE'S, and the row is where Footer's whole
    height sat wrong until 2026-08-24. The band renders 470 against the node's
    480 in BOTH variants (`346:331` Suite, `507:4048` Default, and the homepage
    instance `2336:3503` is identical to them node for node — no override). The
    band is `py-4xl` 64 + the taller of its two columns, and the taller one is
    this list: ten rows on a flat 8 gap. At 27 a row that is 10x27 + 9x8 = 342,
    and 64 + 342 + 64 = 470. At the node's 28 it is 352, and the band is 480.
    Nothing else in the band was short — the left column's content ends at 296
    inside a 352 column and sets no height in either. This paragraph said 292
    until 2026-08-28, when declaring the column's parts sent someone to read
    `346:427` and the four numbers under it added up to 296: 32 + 80 + 80 + 48
    + 56, every one of them now a declared band. Two files had written this
    number down and they disagreed, which is the whole argument for declaring
    it — see the note above Footer's `heading` band. (Not spelled with the
    marker: any line carrying it IS a declaration, so a prose mention is a
    malformed one, and the self-test said so within the minute.)

    What the row was drawn in, read off `507:3253` with get_variable_defs:
    `size/xl` 20 + `weight/bold` + `tracking/tight` + `leading/7` 28 for the
    name, and `Typography/body` — 16/28 regular — for the description. That is
    the h4 role over the body role. The code had `text-lg` 18 and `text-sm` 14
    under a flat `leading-[1.5]`: one type-scale step low on both runs, which is
    what made the line box 27. The letter-spacing was already `tracking-tight`
    -0.4px, so the name's tracking said h4 while its size said title-lg — the
    contradiction that pinned it.

    Proven by injection rather than by reading: setting only these four values
    on HEAD moved the section 470 -> 480.00, the list 342 -> 352.00 and the row
    27 -> 28.00, all exact, and the row's text runs landed within 1-2% of the
    node's own widths (name 94.86 against 97, description 399.52 against 396 —
    Figma and Chrome shape text differently) where before they were 12-13% under.
    Geometry to match: the mark is 24 square at y=2, not 22; the name starts 30
    from the row's left edge, not 26; and the three runs sit on a flat 6, which
    is why the dash has no margin of its own any more. 6 is not a spacing rung,
    same as the 26 and 22 this row already wrote by hand. */}
<li class:list={[className]}>
  <a class="group flex items-center flex-wrap leading-7 gap-[6px] pl-[30px] no-underline text-inherit focus-visible:outline-[length:var(--border-width-2)] focus-visible:outline-solid focus-visible:outline-border-focus focus-visible:outline-offset-[2px] focus-visible:rounded-sm" href={p.href ?? '#'}>
    <span class="w-[24px] h-[24px] shrink-0 flex items-center ml-[-30px]"><img class="w-[24px] h-[24px] block opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-[var(--duration-base)] group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-visible:opacity-100" src={logoSrc} alt="" /></span>
    <span class="text-xl leading-[inherit] font-bold tracking-tight text-text-on-dark-heading flex items-baseline">{p.prefix}<span class="font-normal" style={p.accent ? { color: p.accent } : undefined}>{p.suffix}</span></span>
    {/* Ball's ruling 2026-08-24: `support` at rest, `body` on hover — and the
        DASH follows the description rather than the name, which is what he
        changed in Figma. It used to hover to `text-on-dark-heading`, i.e. white.

        Both now take `text-text-dark-body`. Re-read off `507:3253` after his
        edit, the row binds `text/default/body` where it bound `text/on dark/body`
        before, and the capital-W `White` that was the dash is gone from the set
        entirely. `text/default/body` reads #D4D4D4 in this band's mode, which is
        `--color-text-dark-body` — a FIXED token, not one that flips under
        `.dark`; the one that flips is `--color-text-body`. */}
    <span class="text-base leading-[inherit] text-text-dark-support transition-colors duration-[var(--duration-base)] group-hover:text-text-dark-body group-focus-visible:text-text-dark-body" aria-hidden="true">-</span>
    <span class="text-base leading-[inherit] text-text-dark-support transition-colors duration-[var(--duration-base)] min-w-0 group-hover:text-text-dark-body group-focus-visible:text-text-dark-body">{p.desc}</span>
  </a>
</li>
